WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.620
Speaker 1: The real climate change risks over the&nbsp;
next ten years. Who's telling you the truth about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:15.720
them? There is so much discussion about we've got&nbsp;
eight, ten or 12 years left before total climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:23.280
disaster. There is no greater believer than me in&nbsp;
the climate science believe. I accept the climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.240
science to say I believe in it is not even the&nbsp;
right term because the climate science is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.640
it is, regardless of belief. I accept climate&nbsp;
science. I believe we have to act and I believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:41.040
we have to act now. I also think it's important to&nbsp;
be accurate and sober rather than hyperbolic about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.940
the real risks over the next ten years. So let's&nbsp;
talk about what those are. There are some risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.120
that are overblown, at least when thinking about&nbsp;
the next decade. One of the most important risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.580
of climate change over the next ten years is the&nbsp;
potential for more extreme weather events. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:06.600
have been seeing some of them heatwaves which are&nbsp;
deadly, droughts which can affect food production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:13.740
floods which can create and spread disease and&nbsp;
cause all sorts of other problems. The negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.920
impacts of the extreme weather events as a result&nbsp;
of climate change are very real. They can damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:27.720
infrastructure. They can destroy crops. They can&nbsp;
lead to death and affect human health directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.560
That's a real risk. And it is now and it is&nbsp;
a real risk over the next decade. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.980
significant risk from climate change is the&nbsp;
potential for sea level rise. That's a real risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:46.200
that could have significant impacts on coastal&nbsp;
communities already. And over the next ten years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.020
sea level goes up. It can displace people. It&nbsp;
can cause damage to buildings and infrastructure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.960
Their economic consequences as well as social&nbsp;
consequences. And the unrest that would happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:04.860
if large populations have to start moving because&nbsp;
where they live is underwater. That's that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.800
real risk now and over the next ten years. On&nbsp;
the other hand, let me give you some examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.340
of less important risks, particularly over the&nbsp;
next ten years. Food production is one often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.960
are. Our food production will be destroyed by&nbsp;
climate change and soon is thrown out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:28.200
The best science we have right now does not&nbsp;
actually point that way. Slight global warming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:34.260
could actually be beneficial for agriculture.&nbsp;
Doesn't mean we want it, but it just it is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:39.780
it is. It could be beneficial. Slightly higher&nbsp;
temperatures and slightly higher carbon dioxide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:45.660
levels in the atmosphere could actually improve&nbsp;
crop growth. And there are studies that have shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.520
that under certain conditions, when you have a&nbsp;
little bit higher temperature, a little more CO2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:58.020
you can even increase crop yields like for corn&nbsp;
and wheat. But also you can increase the land area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:04.380
on earth where food can be produced on average.&nbsp;
So right now, you know, in the future, you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:10.080
have a feedback loop that maybe would would point&nbsp;
to it. Being bad for food production. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.700
not actually one that is of of concern right&nbsp;
now. And that's the best science that we have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.140
Another example would be the potential for slight&nbsp;
changes in global temperature. Now, it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.940
important to understand what I'm saying and what&nbsp;
I'm not saying here. A rise in global temperature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:34.320
can have negative impacts. But over the next&nbsp;
ten years, the changes from average global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.600
temperature are likely to be extraordinarily&nbsp;
small or nonexistent. Now distinguish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.440
temperatures being slightly higher on average&nbsp;
is different then acute weather events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:52.620
including heatwaves, which absolutely can be&nbsp;
deadly. But just it being a little bit warmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.780
on average is not actually the disaster over the&nbsp;
next ten years that some believe that it will be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:05.700
Some folks are also concerned about the potential&nbsp;
for climate change to cause widespread loss of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:12.840
biodiversity and accelerate the extinction&nbsp;
of certain species. It is absolutely true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:19.920
that in a feedback loop, climate change can&nbsp;
have negative impacts on biodiversity. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.560
overall risks are considered very low right&nbsp;
now compared to other human activities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:32.100
like the fact that we're destroying habitats&nbsp;
by clearing entire forests as one example and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.220
polluting the air. Okay, so the climate change&nbsp;
component effect on biodiversity over the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:42.120
ten years is much smaller than a lot of these&nbsp;
other things that we are doing. So what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:48.420
takeaway here? The takeaway is it would be&nbsp;
best to have two things happen. Number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.880
we talk about the risks in the realest&nbsp;
scientific terms and if we have a list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:03.960
ten possible disasters. But four of them really&nbsp;
are acute concerns right now. Let's focus on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:12.120
six that are. And number two, let's actually act&nbsp;
on the ones that are the biggest risks, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.620
that's going to have it sort of like the lowest&nbsp;
hanging fruit in the impact that we have if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:23.700
interested in this discussion. The book Precipice&nbsp;
by Toby Ord discusses a lot of what I'm talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:29.940
about in pretty significant detail. Make sure&nbsp;
that you are subscribed on the YouTube channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

